Martian Settlement Autonomy Triad (MSAT): Mathematical Foundations, System Architecture, and Closed‑Form Solutions for Extraterrestrial Bootstrapping

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract

Establishing a permanent human presence on Mars remains fundamentally a logistics problem: every kilogram of habitat, life‑support equipment, and industrial infrastructure delivered from Earth costs millions of dollars and decades of launch capacity. This thesis presents the Martian Settlement Autonomy Triad (MSAT), the first complete, mathematically rigorous blueprint for bootstrapping a habitable settlement using autonomous robotic systems that convert raw Martian regolith into finished infrastructure with 92% local material utilization. Three specialized rover classes—the AWR‑Mars walking industrial rover, the ARC‑Mars construction rover, and the ASR‑Mars logistics rover—operate under the AIADN distributed intelligence to excavate, process, smelt, print, and shield permanent habitats before the first crew arrives.

The work makes five original contributions. First, the Bootstrap Inequality provides a closed‑form lower bound on the Earth‑launched mass required to achieve a target settlement size as a function of the in‑situ resource utilization fraction and habitat geometry. Second, the Triad Interaction Model captures the coupled material‑flow dynamics (steel, regolith, habitat units) as a system of ordinary differential equations under dynamic power allocation. Third, analytic solutions are derived for quadrupedal locomotion gait optimization in 0.38 g via Pontryagin’s Maximum Principle, and for sulfur‑regolith concrete thermodynamics including a Norton‑Bailey creep law parameterised for the Martian diurnal temperature range. Fourth, the AIADN coordination framework is proven to achieve consensus under connected‑graph topologies, and a fault‑tolerant federated learning scheme is extended to operate under the 4–24 minute Earth–Mars latency. Finally, an integrated 27‑sol construction sequence is validated against recent experimental data on vacuum‑compatible 3D‑printed sulfur concrete, demonstrating that the ARC‑8 habitat unit can be completed within one synodic month with a radiation‑shielding factor exceeding 10³.

Economic analysis shows that the mass leverage ratio (Mars‑produced mass per Earth‑supplied mass) grows exponentially to exceed 20 within five Mars years, yielding a positive net present value versus traditional fully‑imported habitats. The thesis closes with a technology roadmap that identifies critical near‑term experiments needed to raise the key subsystems from Technology Readiness Level 3 to flight qualification. By providing provable performance bounds and a complete system‑of‑systems architecture, the MSAT blueprint transforms the challenge of Mars settlement from a logistics exercise into an industrial opportunity.

