3.2 Quadrupedal Kinematics

3.2.1 Forward Kinematics

Each leg of the AWR‑Mars is a 6‑DOF serial chain with three hip joints (yaw, roll, pitch), one knee joint, and two ankle joints (pitch, roll). The Denavit‑Hartenberg (DH) parameters are given in Table 3.2.

Table 3.2 — DH parameters for a single leg.

Joint \theta d a \alpha
Hip Yaw \theta_1 0 0 −90°
Hip Roll \theta_2 0 L_1 0
Hip Pitch \theta_3 0 0 90°
Knee \theta_4 0 L_2 0
Ankle Pitch \theta_5 0 0 −90°
Ankle Roll \theta_6 0 L_3 0

The homogeneous transformation from the hip frame to the foot frame is:

T_6^0 = \prod_{i=1}^{6} T_i^{i-1} \tag{3.1}

where each elementary transformation has the standard DH form:

T_i^{i-1} = \begin{bmatrix}
\cos\theta_i & -\sin\theta_i \cos\alpha_i & \sin\theta_i \sin\alpha_i & a_i \cos\theta_i \\
\sin\theta_i & \cos\theta_i \cos\alpha_i & -\cos\theta_i \sin\alpha_i & a_i \sin\theta_i \\
0 & \sin\alpha_i & \cos\alpha_i & d_i \\
0 & 0 & 0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}

3.2.2 Inverse Kinematics (Closed‑Form Solution)

Given a desired foot position \mathbf{p} = [x, y, z]^T in the hip frame, the joint angles admit the following analytic solution:

\theta_1 = \text{atan2}(y, x) \tag{3.2}

D = \frac{x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - L_1^2 - L_2^2 - L_3^2}{2 L_2 L_3} \tag{3.3}

\theta_3 = \text{atan2}\!\left(\sqrt{1 - D^2},\ D\right) \tag{3.4}

\theta_2 = \text{atan2}(z, \sqrt{x^2 + y^2}) - \text{atan2}(L_3 \sin\theta_3,\ L_2 + L_3 \cos\theta_3) \tag{3.5}

The ankle joints \theta_5, \theta_6 are solved to maintain a specified foot orientation relative to the terrain normal. The closed‑form nature of this solution permits real‑time computation at the 1 kHz control loop rate on radiation‑hardened embedded processors.

3.3 Dynamics and Whole‑Body Control

3.3.1 Lagrangian Formulation

The full 24‑DOF dynamics of the AWR‑Mars are described by the standard manipulator equation:

M(\mathbf{q}) \ddot{\mathbf{q}} + C(\mathbf{q}, \dot{\mathbf{q}}) \dot{\mathbf{q}} + G(\mathbf{q}) = \boldsymbol{\tau} + J^T(\mathbf{q}) \mathbf{F}_{\text{ext}} \tag{3.6}

where \mathbf{q} \in \mathbb{R}^{24} is the vector of joint angles, M is the configuration‑dependent mass matrix, C captures Coriolis and centrifugal terms, G is the gravity vector (scaled by 0.378), \boldsymbol{\tau} is the vector of joint torques, J is the stacked foot Jacobians, and \mathbf{F}_{\text{ext}} are the ground reaction forces.

3.3.2 Hierarchical Whole‑Body Control

The AWR‑Mars employs a two‑layer hierarchical control architecture. The top layer solves a model predictive optimization that minimizes control effort while enforcing dynamic balance, using a simplified centroidal dynamics model to plan ground reaction forces (GRFs) over a 2 s receding horizon. The bottom layer solves a quadratic program at 1 kHz that tracks these GRFs while respecting joint torque limits, friction cones, and kinematic constraints:

\min_{\boldsymbol{\tau}, \mathbf{F}} \|\boldsymbol{\tau}\|^2_{W_\tau} + \|\mathbf{F}\|^2_{W_F}

subject to:

M\ddot{\mathbf{q}} + C\dot{\mathbf{q}} + G = \boldsymbol{\tau} + J^T \mathbf{F}

F_i \in \mathcal{FC}_i \quad \text{(friction cone)}

\boldsymbol{\tau}_{\min} \leq \boldsymbol{\tau} \leq \boldsymbol{\tau}_{\max}

This architecture has been validated in planetary‑analog field tests by Stark et al. (2025), where a quadruped using adaptive model‑based control successfully traversed 700 m of volcanic terrain while handling uncertain and deformable ground properties.

3.4 Gait Optimization in 0.38 g

Traditional quadrupedal gait patterns are optimized for terrestrial gravity. The reduced gravity of Mars changes the trade space: slower stride frequencies suffice to maintain the same Froude number, reducing joint power, while lower ground reaction forces reduce the risk of sinkage in loose regolith.

The energy‑optimal walking gait is found by solving the following optimal control problem:

\min_{\mathbf{F}_i(t)} J = \int_0^T \left( \sum_{i=1}^{24} \tau_i^2 + \lambda \|\mathbf{p}_{\text{COM}} - \mathbf{p}_{\text{des}}\|^2 \right) dt \tag{3.7}

subject to the no‑slip condition F_{\text{tangential}} \leq \mu F_{\text{normal}} and the torque limits.

Applying Pontryagin’s Maximum Principle yields the optimal foot‑force distribution law:

F_i = \frac{1/d_i^2}{\sum_{j=1}^4 1/d_j^2} \cdot (m g_M + m a_{\text{COM}}) \tag{3.8}

where d_i is the distance from the COM projection to foot i. This law distributes the total required force among the supporting feet in inverse proportion to their squared distance from the COM, minimizing the joint torques that must be generated. The optimal walking frequency follows from the linearized spring‑loaded inverted pendulum model:

f_{\text{opt}} = \frac{1}{2\pi} \sqrt{\frac{g_M}{l_{\text{leg}}}} \approx 0.5\ \text{Hz} \quad \text{for } l_{\text{leg}} = 1.5\ \text{m} \tag{3.9}

3.5 Quick‑Swap Tool Ecosystem (QSI‑1)

The AWR‑Mars mounts interchangeable tool heads via the QSI‑1 (Quick‑Swap Interface – Standard) mechanical, electrical, and data connector. The interface achieves a clamping force of 2 000 N via a wedge‑lock mechanism with a mechanical advantage of 3.2, requiring only 625 N of motor force. Gold‑gold electrical contacts rated for 50 A at 400 V yield a connector efficiency of 99.9%. Optical alignment to ±3 µm is achieved through active Hall‑effect sensing feedback.

