3.6 Carbothermic Steel Production

3.6.1 Thermodynamic Foundation

The production of structural iron from Martian hematite (Fe₂O₃) proceeds via the well‑established carbothermic reduction:

\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3 + 3\text{CO} \rightarrow 2\text{Fe} + 3\text{CO}_2 \quad \Delta H = -24.8\ \text{kJ mol}^{-1}

Experimental validation by Nababan and Rhamdhani (2025) demonstrated that pure metallic iron forms at 1 060 °C from the Mars Global Simulant (MGS‑1) under Mars‑relevant pressures of 4–10 mbar, and liquid Fe‑Si alloy is produced above 1 400 °C. This is the first experimental confirmation of the carbothermic pathway under simultaneous low‑pressure and high‑temperature conditions that replicate the Martian environment, and it directly anchors the AWR‑Mars foundry design.

Table 3.4 — Carbothermic reduction experimental results (MGS‑1 simulant, 7 mbar).

Temperature Range Product Physical State
1 000–1 070 °C Pure metallic iron Solid
1 070–1 150 °C Fe with minor Si Partial smelting
1 150–1 450 °C Fe‑Si alloy Liquid + molten slag

3.6.2 Energy Model

The specific energy required per kilogram of iron produced is:

E_{\text{Fe}} = \frac{\Delta H}{M_{\text{Fe}}} \cdot \frac{1}{\eta_{\text{thermal}}} \approx 6.2\ \text{MJ kg}^{-1}

where \eta_{\text{thermal}} \approx 0.65 accounts for furnace losses. With 15 kW electrical input to the IC‑1 crucible, the steady‑state production rate is:

\dot{m}_{\text{Fe}} = \frac{P_{\text{elec}} \cdot \eta_{\text{thermal}}}{E_{\text{Fe}}} \approx 3.5\ \text{kg h}^{-1}

A single AWR‑Mars, operating the IC‑1 for 8 h per sol, can therefore produce approximately 28 kg of iron per sol, rising to ~100 kg of structural steel per sol once the full foundry core is operational and multiple rovers contribute.

3.6.3 Carbon Supply Chain

The carbon required for reduction is obtained from the Martian atmosphere via the MOXIE‑heritage solid‑oxide electrolysis process. At 800 °C, CO₂ is split into CO and O₂ with a demonstrated conversion efficiency of >98%. The CO stream is fed directly into the reduction furnace, while the O₂ by‑product is compressed and stored.

3.7 Laser Additive Manufacturing

For tool‑head fabrication and spare‑part production, the AWR‑Mars employs a laser‑based directed energy deposition (DED) system operating at 2 kW fiber‑laser power. The melt‑pool dynamics are governed by the quasi‑static heat equation:

d_{\text{melt}} = \sqrt{\frac{2\alpha P_{\text{laser}}}{\pi \kappa v (T_m - T_0)}} \tag{3.10}

where \alpha = \kappa/(\rho c_p) is the thermal diffusivity of the metal powder, v = 0.01 m s⁻¹ is the deposition scan speed, T_m = 1\,500 °C is the melting point of the steel alloy, and T_0 = −63 °C is the ambient temperature. The calculated melt depth of approximately 5 mm enables single‑pass deposition with full layer fusion.

Recent studies on laser additive manufacturing in vacuum and reduced gravity confirm that DED with wire feedstock maintains stable melt‑pool geometry even in near‑vacuum, provided that active process control maintains a continuous liquid‑metal bridge between the wire and the melt pool. The AWR‑Mars laser head therefore includes a closed‑loop vision system that monitors the melt‑pool width and adjusts laser power at 100 Hz to compensate for variations in ambient pressure and gravity vector.

3.8 Molten Regolith Electrolysis – A Parallel Pathway

While the MSAT baseline employs carbothermic reduction for iron production, recent advances in molten regolith electrolysis (MRE) offer a complementary pathway that eliminates the need for a carbon reductant. The 2025 study by Guan et al. demonstrated the separation of oxygen and metals from a Mars soil simulant via molten salt electrolysis, producing ferrosilicon alloys suitable for construction materials while releasing oxygen as a valuable by‑product. This process requires only electrical energy and would integrate seamlessly with the Kilopower nuclear power system once higher electrical power (≥100 kWe) is available. The MRE pathway is therefore included in the MSAT technology roadmap as a Phase 3 upgrade.

3.9 Self‑Replication Pathway

The ultimate measure of an autonomous industrial system is its capacity for self‑replication. The AWR‑Mars is designed with a staged pathway toward this goal:

· Year 1–2: Fabricate spare tool heads and replacement actuators using the laser AM system, drawing on Earth‑supplied alloying elements (Cr, Ni) for critical components.
· Year 3–4: Manufacture structural chassis components (leg segments, frame members) from locally produced steel. Assemble the first Mars‑built AWR‑Mars unit with approximately 70% local mass fraction.
· Year 5–7: Achieve full rover reproduction at 90%+ local mass fraction, with only semiconductor electronics and rare‑earth magnets imported from Earth.
· Year 7+: The rover population grows exponentially, constrained only by available power and raw material.

3.10 Thermal and Power Management

The AWR‑Mars operates in an environment where the ambient temperature can drop to −125 °C while the internal electronics must be maintained between −40 °C and +85 °C. The thermal balance is:

C_{\text{rover}} \frac{dT}{dt} = Q_{\text{gen}} - Q_{\text{loss}} \tag{3.11}

where Q_{\text{gen}} = \sum (1 - \eta_i) P_i is the waste heat from actuators, processors, and tools, and the heat loss term includes both radiative and residual convective coupling:

Q_{\text{loss}} = hA(T - T_{\text{env}}) + \epsilon \sigma A (T^4 - T_{\text{env}}^4)

Under nominal operating conditions with 2 kW of waste heat, the rover’s multi‑layer insulation (MLI) blanket and selective surface coatings maintain the internal avionics bay at 20 °C with zero active heating. During extended idle periods in the cold Martian night, a small fraction (< 5%) of the battery capacity is diverted to resistive heating elements that keep critical electronics above the −40 °C cold‑start threshold.

