4.2 Sulfur‑Regolith Concrete: Updated Mechanics

4.2.1 Material Composition and the Waterless Advantage

Sulfur‑regolith concrete (SRC) is formed by melting elemental sulfur (melting point 115 °C) and mixing it with graded regolith aggregate at approximately 20–25 wt% sulfur binder. Upon cooling, the sulfur crystallises into a monolithic matrix that binds the aggregate particles without any hydration reaction. This waterless curing mechanism is the single most important advantage of SRC for Martian application: the planet’s surface is desiccated, and any water imported or extracted must be reserved for life support and propellant production.

Recent experimental work at Louisiana State University in collaboration with NASA Marshall Space Flight Center (Banijamali et al., 2025) has provided the first comprehensive characterisation of 3D‑printed SRC using high‑fidelity Martian regolith simulant under vacuum and temperature‑variable conditions. The study confirms that SRC specimens printed under vacuum exhibit higher mechanical performance than equivalent Portland cement concrete (PCC) specimens under the same conditions, and that the material gains approximately 85% of its ultimate compressive strength within 12 h of deposition—a curing rate orders of magnitude faster than PCC and ideally suited to a 27‑sol construction cadence.

4.2.2 Compressive Strength Model with Gravity Scaling

The unconfined compressive strength of SRC follows an empirical power‑law dependence on the sulfur‑to‑aggregate ratio (s/a) and the water‑to‑sulfur ratio (w/s, here identically zero):

\sigma_c = 45\ \text{MPa} \cdot \left(\frac{s/a}{0.30}\right)^{0.4} \cdot \left(\frac{0.20}{w/s}\right)^{0.3} , \quad (w/s \rightarrow 0) \tag{4.1}

At the optimal mix design of s/a = 0.30, the baseline compressive strength on Earth is approximately 45 MPa. Independent confirmation from IIT‑Madras (2025) demonstrates that sulfur‑based waterless concrete using Martian simulant achieves strengths exceeding 50 MPa under Earth gravity. Under Martian gravity (0.378 g), the effective structural strength is amplified by a factor of approximately 2.65 for gravity‑dominated load paths, yielding an effective design strength of approximately 120 MPa for self‑weight and overburden calculations.

4.2.3 DCPD Modification: Strength Enhancement and Sublimation Suppression

A critical finding of the 2024–2025 experimental campaigns is the efficacy of Dicyclopentadiene (DCPD) as a modifier for extraterrestrial SRC. DCPD is a hydrocarbon monomer that copolymerises with molten sulfur, forming a cross‑linked polysulfide network that:

1. Increases flexural strength by up to 44% relative to unmodified SRC.
2. Suppresses the sulfur sublimation rate under vacuum and elevated temperatures—a failure mode unique to the Martian near‑vacuum environment where the vapour pressure of sulfur becomes significant above approximately 80 °C.

Banijamali et al. (2025) report that DCPD‑modified SRC specimens demonstrated "significantly enhanced mechanical strength and minimized sublimation rate under vacuum and elevated temperatures". The optimal DCPD concentration range is 3–10 wt% of the sulfur fraction. The ARC‑Mars therefore specifies a DCPD doping module integrated into the heated mixing chamber, with a target concentration of 5 wt%. The DCPD feedstock is among the small fraction (< 8%) of Earth‑sourced consumables, but its mass is modest: approximately 1.2 kg per ARC‑8 habitat, or 48 kg for the full Phase 1 settlement.

4.2.4 Inter‑Layer Bonding and the Print Cadence Constraint

Extrusion‑based additive manufacturing of SRC introduces a thermal history dependence that directly constrains the construction sequence. Each deposited layer fuses to the substrate only if the interface temperature exceeds the glass‑transition temperature of the sulfur matrix (approximately 115 °C). If the substrate cools below this threshold before the next layer is deposited, the inter‑layer bond is compromised.

The 2024 experimental data quantify this effect precisely: extended inter‑layer delays during 3D printing of Martian SRC can reduce flexural strength by up to 53% relative to continuously deposited specimens. The strength loss follows an approximately exponential decay with inter‑layer time:

\frac{\sigma_f(\Delta t)}{\sigma_f(0)} \approx 1 - 0.53 \cdot (1 - e^{-\Delta t / \tau_{\text{bond}}}) \tag{4.2}

where \tau_{\text{bond}} \approx 90\ \text{s} is the characteristic cooling time constant of a 10 mm layer in the Martian ambient environment.

This finding has profound implications for the ARC‑Mars construction protocol. At a continuous vertical print rate of 0.5 m h⁻¹ and a layer height of 10 mm, the inter‑layer time is approximately 72 s—within the safe window. However, any interruption exceeding approximately 3 min requires active re‑heating of the substrate via the microwave sintering head (RC‑M1) to restore interface temperature above 130 °C before deposition can resume. The 27‑sol construction sequence (Section 4.5) incorporates this constraint by scheduling all interruptions (ASR docking, tool changes) at pre‑planned layer boundaries where the microwave re‑heating protocol is automatically executed.

4.2.5 Thermomechanical Endurance Under Annual Cycling

The long‑term structural integrity of an SRC habitat depends on its ability to survive the full Martian thermal year without tensile cracking. A 2025 numerical study by TU Braunschweig modelled a regolith‑covered sulfur‑concrete cupola under internal heating to 290 K (17 °C) and external exposure to the annual temperature cycle (approximately −60 °C average to −120 °C minimum). The key findings were:

· The regolith overburden acts as a thermal buffer that reduces the amplitude of the temperature wave at the concrete shell to negligible levels (< 5 °C diurnal).
· The cupola survives the full annual thermal cycle without tensile failure, provided that the concrete shell is continuously supported by the regolith berm.
· The primary loading mode is not thermal stress but the internal pressurisation of 101.3 kPa, for which the 300 mm concrete shell provides a safety factor exceeding 8.

These results validate the fundamental design philosophy of the ARC‑8: a pressure vessel of modest thickness (300 mm) embedded within a 2.5 m regolith overburden that simultaneously provides radiation shielding and thermal stability.

4.3 Thermoplastic Creep and the Bailey‑Norton Law

4.3.1 Creep Mechanism in Sulfur Concrete

Sulfur concrete is a thermoplastic composite: the sulfur binder, even in its solid state below 115 °C, exhibits viscoelastic deformation under sustained load. This creep behaviour is distinct from the drying shrinkage and hydration creep of Portland cement concrete, and it must be explicitly modelled for a structure that will be continuously pressurised for decades.

The classical Bailey‑Norton (power‑law) creep model provides a constitutive relation between creep strain rate \dot{\epsilon}_c, applied stress \sigma, and absolute temperature T:

\dot{\epsilon}_c = A \cdot \sigma^n \cdot e^{-Q/RT} \tag{4.3}

where the parameters for sulfur concrete, drawn from experimental data on sulfur‑bonded civil engineering materials (2025 review), are:

· A = 2.3 \times 10^{-7}\ \text{MPa}^{-n}\,\text{s}^{-1} (pre‑exponential factor)
· n = 3.2 (stress exponent)
· Q = 85\ \text{kJ}\,\text{mol}^{-1} (activation energy for creep)
· R = 8.314\ \text{J}\,\text{mol}^{-1}\,\text{K}^{-1} (universal gas constant)

At the mean operating temperature of the ARC‑8 concrete shell—approximately 255 K after equilibration with the regolith overburden—and at a design stress of 5 MPa (the compressive stress from internal pressure with safety factor 8), the creep rate evaluates to:

\dot{\epsilon}_c(255\ \text{K}, 5\ \text{MPa}) \approx 1.2 \times 10^{-12}\ \text{s}^{-1} \approx 3.8 \times 10^{-5}\ \text{year}^{-1}

Integrated over a 10‑year service interval, the cumulative creep strain is approximately 0.038%—negligible for structural purposes. Even under a worst‑case scenario where the shell temperature rises to 290 K (e.g., during an extended internal heating anomaly), the creep rate increases to approximately 10^{-10}\ \text{s}^{-1}, yielding 0.32% strain per Mars year—still acceptable with the pre‑stress system described below.

4.3.2 Steel Tendon Pre‑Stress for Creep Compensation

To counteract both long‑term creep and the coefficient of thermal expansion (CTE) mismatch between SRC (CTE ≈ 18 × 10⁻⁶ K⁻¹) and the steel reinforcement (CTE ≈ 12 × 10⁻⁶ K⁻¹), the ARC‑8 design incorporates a system of circumferential steel tendons post‑tensioned during construction. The required pre‑stress level is:

\sigma_p = \alpha_{\text{SRC}}\,\Delta T\,E_s + \frac{\dot{\epsilon}_c\,\Delta t\,E_c}{1 - \nu^2} \tag{4.4}

where \alpha_{\text{SRC}} = 18 \times 10^{-6}\ \text{K}^{-1}, \Delta T = 50\ \text{K} is the maximum diurnal temperature differential at the wall mid‑depth (conservative, given the thermal buffering of the overburden), E_s = 200\ \text{GPa} and E_c = 30\ \text{GPa} are the elastic moduli of steel and concrete respectively, \nu = 0.2 is Poisson’s ratio, and \Delta t \approx 10\ \text{years} is the design interval.

Evaluating Equation (4.4) yields \sigma_p \approx 180\ \text{MPa}. This is achieved with 12 mm‑diameter high‑strength steel tendons tensioned to 20 kN each, spaced at 90° intervals around the dome circumference (four tendons per ARC‑8 unit). The tendons are fabricated by the AWR‑Mars foundry from locally produced steel, closing the material loop within the triad.

4.4 Additive Construction Dynamics

4.4.1 Extrusion Rheology

The SRC melt behaves as a Bingham plastic during extrusion: it requires a finite yield stress \tau_y to initiate flow, after which it exhibits a linear relationship between shear stress and shear rate with apparent viscosity \mu. The pressure drop across a circular nozzle of radius R and length L is:

\Delta P = \frac{8 \mu L Q}{\pi R^4} + \frac{2 \tau_y L}{R} \tag{4.5}

For the ARC‑Mars extrusion system: \mu \approx 10^4\ \text{Pa·s} (measured for SRC at 135 °C), \tau_y \approx 200\ \text{Pa}, R = 25\ \text{mm}, L = 0.5\ \text{m}, and a volumetric flow rate Q = 2.5\ \text{kg}\,\text{min}^{-1} / 2\,000\ \text{kg}\,\text{m}^{-3} \approx 1.25 \times 10^{-3}\ \text{m}^3\,\text{min}^{-1}. Evaluating Equation (4.5):

\Delta P \approx 1.2\ \text{MPa}

This pressure is readily achievable with a progressive‑cavity pump driven by a 2 kW electric motor—well within the ARC‑Mars power budget of 8 kW nominal during printing operations.

4.4.2 Laser Vitrification: The Exterior Glaze

The outermost layer of the ARC‑8 habitat (Layer 7 in the wall cross‑section) is a vitrified glass coating formed by the RC‑L1 laser head scanning across the finished regolith surface. This process, known as Laser Vitreous Multi‑material Transformation, has been advanced to prototype stage by ICON under NASA’s Moon‑to‑Mars Planetary Autonomous Construction Technology (MMPACT) programme. A high‑power laser (≈10 kW) melts the surface regolith to a depth of approximately 5–10 mm; upon resolidification, the material forms a continuous glassy layer with compressive strength exceeding 100 MPa and near‑zero gas permeability. The glaze serves as the primary gas barrier, supplementing the Kevlar/polymer liner (Earth‑supplied) that provides redundancy.

The energy required per square metre of vitrified surface is:

E_{\text{vitrify}} = \frac{\rho\,c_p\,\Delta T\,d_{\text{melt}}}{\eta_{\text{laser}}} \tag{4.6}

where \rho \approx 2\,000\ \text{kg}\,\text{m}^{-3} is the sintered regolith density, c_p \approx 800\ \text{J}\,\text{kg}^{-1}\,\text{K}^{-1}, \Delta T \approx 1\,200\ \text{K} (to reach the melting point of approximately 1 150 °C), d_{\text{melt}} \approx 8\ \text{mm}, and \eta_{\text{laser}} \approx 0.4 accounts for optical and thermal losses. Evaluating:

E_{\text{vitrify}} \approx 48\ \text{MJ}\,\text{m}^{-2}

For the ARC‑8 dome exterior surface area of approximately 100 m² (hemispherical cap of radius 4 m), the total vitrification energy is approximately 4.8 GJ, or approximately 1 333 kWh. At 10 kW laser power and 40% duty cycle, this requires approximately 330 h of laser operation—spread over three sols (Sol 25–27) with continuous operation during daylight hours.

