For comparison, the Earth’s atmosphere provides approximately 1 030 g cm⁻² at sea level. The attenuation of the Martian surface radiation field, as modelled in Section 2.4, reduces the internal dose from 0.67 mSv day⁻¹ to approximately 0.0045 mSv day⁻¹—a factor exceeding 10³ and well within the NASA astronaut career limit of 600 mSv.

4.5.4 Micrometeorite Impact Survivability

A 1 cm‑diameter micrometeorite travelling at 10 km s⁻¹ delivers a peak shock pressure of:

P_{\text{peak}} = \rho_p v_p c_s \approx 3\,000 \times 10\,000 \times 4\,000 \approx 120\ \text{GPa}

The spallation damage depth is estimated from shock‑wave decay:

d_{\text{spall}} \approx \frac{c_s \tau}{2} \ln\!\left(\frac{P_{\text{peak}}}{P_{\text{spall}}}\right) \tag{4.8}

With \tau \approx 2\ \mu\text{s} (pulse duration for a 1 cm impactor at 10 km s⁻¹), c_s \approx 4\,000\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}, P_{\text{spall}} \approx 100\ \text{MPa} (dynamic tensile strength of SRC):

d_{\text{spall}} \approx \frac{4\,000 \times 2 \times 10^{-6}}{2} \times \ln(1.2 \times 10^6) \approx 0.4\ \text{m}

This is less than the 2.5 m total overburden thickness. The vitrified glaze and regolith shield absorb the impact energy before the concrete pressure vessel is reached. The probability of a > 1 cm impactor striking a given ARC‑8 unit in a given Mars year is estimated at < 10⁻⁴, based on the Grün impact flux model scaled to Mars orbital distance.

