4.7 Comparative Material Pathways

While SRC is the baseline material, two alternative pathways warrant discussion.

4.7.1 Geopolymer‑Based High‑Performance Martian Concrete

Recent work at UTS (2025) developed a cement‑free geopolymer‑based high‑performance Martian concrete (HPMC) using Martian soil simulant. Optimal results—with 50% regolith simulant incorporation—yielded a 7‑day compressive strength of 62.8 MPa. Curing at 40 °C produced superior early and long‑term strengths, while sub‑zero temperatures (−20 °C to 0 °C) diminished strength due to reduced aluminosilicate dissolution. Carbonation curing with steel fibre addition further improved strength to 98.3 MPa after 60 days, although long‑term CO₂ exposure degraded the steel fibres.

The principal drawback of geopolymer HPMC for the MSAT application is its dependence on alkaline activators (sodium silicate, sodium hydroxide) that are not readily available on Mars. Until an ISRU pathway for alkali production is established, geopolymer concrete remains a future option rather than a baseline.

4.7.2 Cold Sintering Process

The Cold Sintering Process (CSP) achieves rapid consolidation (15–75 min) at only 80–200 °C and 50–250 MPa, producing ceramic‑like materials with compressive strengths ranging from 42.6 to 193.9 MPa. A 2025 case study of CSP applied to Mars regolith simulant demonstrated its promise as an ISRU technique, although the requirement for high uniaxial pressure limits its applicability to pre‑fabricated block production rather than in‑situ extrusion. The MSAT technology roadmap incorporates CSP as a potential Phase 3 upgrade for block fabrication, complementing the SRC extrusion baseline.

