5.2 Excavation Dynamics: Ultrasonic Vibration Assistance

5.2.1 Classical Terramechanics Model

The excavation force required to cut regolith at depth h with a bucket of width w follows the logarithmic‑spiral failure model introduced in Section 2.2, adapted here for the ASR‑Mars bucket‑drum excavator:

F_{\text{dig}} = (c A + W \tan\phi) \left(1 + \frac{v^2}{2 g_M d}\right) \tag{5.1}

where c \approx 1\ \text{kPa} is the regolith cohesion (dry, unconsolidated), A = w \times d = 0.5 \times 0.2 = 0.1\ \text{m}^2 is the bucket frontal area, W = \rho g_M V_{\text{bucket}} \approx 1\,600 \times 3.711 \times 0.01 \approx 59\ \text{N} is the weight of the excavated material, \phi \approx 35^\circ is the internal friction angle, and v \approx 0.1\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1} is the cutting speed.

Evaluating: F_{\text{dig}} \approx (100 + 59 \times 0.70) \times (1 + \frac{0.01}{2 \times 3.711 \times 0.2}) \approx 141\ \text{N} static component, rising to approximately 800 N when dynamic effects and bucket‑drum inertia are included—within the 2 kN capability of the excavator arm.

5.2.2 Ultrasonic Vibration Force Reduction

A 2025 parabolic‑flight study of an ultrasonically vibrating blade (nominally 20 kHz, 10–40 µm amplitude) in regolith simulant under simulated lunar and Martian gravity demonstrated significant reductions in penetration force. Key findings include:

· Continuous 20 kHz vibration reduces penetration force across all tested gravity levels (Earth, Mars, Moon).
· Pulsed vibration (duty cycle 50%) achieves force reduction comparable to continuous vibration while halving the electrical power consumption.
· Excessive vibro‑fluidisation (at amplitudes > 40 µm) can increase force by disrupting the granular force chains that support the cutting edge.

The ASR‑Mars therefore incorporates an ultrasonic transducer in the bucket‑drum assembly, operating at 20 kHz with 20 µm amplitude and a 50% duty cycle. Based on the parabolic‑flight data, this configuration yields a force reduction of approximately 35% relative to the non‑vibratory baseline, reducing the effective F_{\text{dig}} from 800 N to approximately 520 N and proportionally lowering actuator torque and energy consumption.

5.3 Material Processing Chain

The ASR‑Mars integrates three sequential processing stages within its chassis, transforming raw regolith into the four output streams defined in the triad material model.

5.3.1 Vibratory Screening: Rosin‑Rammler Separation Model

The three‑stage vibratory sieve classifies particles by size. The probability that a particle of diameter d passes through a screen of aperture size d_{50} is modelled by the Rosin‑Rammler (Weibull) distribution:

P(d) = 1 - \exp\!\left[-\left(\frac{d}{d_{50}}\right)^m\right] \tag{5.2}

where d_{50} = 100\ \mu\text{m} is the cut size for the finest screen and m = 2.5 is the sharpness index (higher values indicate sharper separation). The three screens are configured as:

Screen Aperture Output Stream Mass Fraction
Stage 1 (coarse) 5 mm Structural aggregate ~40%
Stage 2 (medium) 500 µm Sintering fines ~30%
Stage 3 (fine) 100 µm Sulfate concentrate ~20%
Residual (pan) — Waste tailings ~10%

The total throughput at 50 Hz vibration frequency with a 0.5 m² screen area is approximately 500 kg h⁻¹, matching the excavation rate.

5.3.2 Magnetic Separation: Hematite Enrichment

The structural aggregate stream (Stage 1) is passed over a magnetic drum separator operating at a surface field strength of 1 T. The magnetic force on a hematite particle in the gradient field is:

F_m = \frac{\chi V}{\mu_0} (B \cdot \nabla) B \tag{5.3}

For hematite (Fe₂O₃): magnetic susceptibility \chi \approx 0.4 (SI), particle volume V \approx 5.2 \times 10^{-16}\ \text{m}^3 (for a 10 µm particle), B \approx 1\ \text{T}, and gradient \nabla B \approx 10^3\ \text{T}\,\text{m}^{-1}. Evaluating:

F_m \approx \frac{0.4 \times 5.2 \times 10^{-16}}{4\pi \times 10^{-7}} \times 10^3 \approx 1.7 \times 10^{-7}\ \text{N}

The competing aerodynamic drag force in the Martian atmosphere (CO₂ at 600 Pa, dynamic viscosity \mu \approx 10^{-5}\ \text{Pa·s}, particle velocity 1 m s⁻¹) is:

F_d = 3\pi \mu d_p v \approx 3\pi \times 10^{-5} \times 10 \times 10^{-6} \times 1 \approx 9.4 \times 10^{-10}\ \text{N}

The ratio F_m / F_d \approx 180 confirms that magnetic separation is highly effective even for fine particles in the rarefied Martian atmosphere. The enriched hematite concentrate (≈35% Fe) is routed to the AWR‑Mars foundry core via the pneumatic transfer system.

A 2025 review of dry magnetic separation techniques for deep‑space mineral resources confirms the viability of this approach and identifies the key engineering challenge as managing the electrostatic adhesion of fines to the drum surface. The ASR‑Mars addresses this with an integrated electrodynamic dust shield (Section 5.5).

5.3.3 Sulfur Extraction from Sulfates

The sulfate concentrate (Stage 3) undergoes a three‑step thermal decomposition sequence to extract elemental sulfur for use as the SRC binder:

1. Dehydration: CaSO₄·2H₂O → CaSO₄ + 2H₂O (at 200 °C; water recovered and stored).
2. Decomposition: CaSO₄ → CaO + SO₃ (at 1 200 °C).
3. Reduction: SO₃ + C → SO₂ + CO (at 900 °C); 2SO₂ + 4H₂ → S₂ + 4H₂O (at 400 °C, catalytic).

The overall chain efficiency is:

\eta_S = \eta_{\text{thermal}} \cdot \eta_{\text{reduction}} \cdot \eta_{\text{capture}} \approx 0.70 \times 0.80 \times 0.90 \approx 0.50

Given a sulfate abundance of 5–10 wt% in Martian regolith, the recoverable sulfur yield is approximately 2.5–5.0 kg per 100 kg of processed regolith—sufficient to binder the SRC mix at the optimal s/a ratio of 0.30.

5.4 Pneumatic Conveying in the Martian Atmosphere

5.4.1 Feasibility and Energy Analysis

The transport of granular regolith from the ASR‑Mars to the ARC‑Mars construction site via pneumatic pipeline leverages the CO₂ atmosphere as the working fluid—a concept now under formal study by ESA through the Aeolis project (2025). The Aeolis study adapts dust‑transport models to evaluate low‑pressure‑gradient regolith handling, concluding that suction devices can efficiently capture fine regolith for additive manufacturing while blowers and wind tunnels enable transport and size sorting.

The pressure drop for dilute‑phase pneumatic conveying in a pipe of length L, diameter D, with gas velocity u, follows the Darcy‑Weisbach equation:

\Delta p = f_D \frac{L}{D} \frac{\rho_g u^2}{2} \tag{5.4}

where f_D \approx 0.02 is the Darcy friction factor for turbulent flow in a smooth pipe, \rho_g \approx 0.02\ \text{kg}\,\text{m}^{-3} is the density of CO₂ at 600 Pa and −60 °C, and u \approx 20\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1} is the transport velocity (approximately 20× the saltation threshold for 100 µm particles in Mars gravity). For L = 10\ \text{m} (the maximum distance from ASR docking port to ARC‑Mars hopper) and D = 0.1\ \text{m}:

\Delta p \approx 0.02 \times \frac{10}{0.1} \times \frac{0.02 \times 20^2}{2} \approx 0.8\ \text{kPa}

This modest pressure drop—less than double the ambient Martian pressure—is readily generated by a compact centrifugal blower consuming approximately 200 W. Pneumatic conveying has been experimentally validated under reduced‑gravity conditions at NASA Kennedy Space Center, confirming its viability as a low‑energy, low‑failure‑mode regolith delivery system for ISRU applications.

5.4.2 Particle Saltation and Sorting

A beneficial side effect of pneumatic conveying is natural aerodynamic sorting: smaller (lighter) particles are more easily entrained and travel faster, while larger particles lag or settle. This effect, known as saltation sorting, can be exploited to pre‑classify the regolith stream en route. The ESA Aeolis study specifically identifies this mechanism as a means to "naturally sort regolith by size as smaller grains are more easily lifted and transported than larger ones". The ASR‑Mars pneumatic system incorporates a cyclonic separator at the ARC‑Mars receiver end that removes the coarse fraction (> 100 µm) for structural aggregate use, while the fines continue to the sintering hopper.

5.5 Dust Mitigation: Integrated Electrodynamic and Mechanical Strategies

Martian regolith is electrostatically charged by triboelectric effects during excavation and transport, causing it to adhere tenaciously to surfaces. The ASR‑Mars employs a multi‑layered dust‑mitigation strategy:

1. Electrodynamic Dust Shield (EDS): Interdigitated electrodes on the solar panels, camera lenses, and magnetic separator drum are driven by a three‑phase AC waveform (≈1 kV, 10 Hz), generating a travelling electric field that repels charged dust particles. Laboratory testing in Mars‑simulation chambers has demonstrated removal efficiencies exceeding 90%.
2. Positive‑pressure purging: During docking with the ARC‑Mars, the connector interface is briefly pressurised with filtered CO₂ to eject dust from the mating surfaces before the QSI‑1 umbilical is engaged.
3. Mechanical vibration: The vibratory screens serve a dual purpose—material classification and self‑cleaning. During dust‑storm recovery, the screens are driven at their resonant frequency (50 Hz) without material feed, shedding accumulated dust through inertial acceleration.

The DUST‑SHROUD protocol for major dust storms (visibility < 100 m) commands the ASR‑Mars to anchor to the nearest permanent structure, deploy the EDS in continuous mode, and enter a low‑power hibernation state until the storm passes—typically 2–5 sols. Post‑storm recovery includes a full sensor calibration sequence and a self‑cleaning cycle before normal operations resume.

5.6 Logistics Optimization: The Multi‑Depot Traveling Purchaser Problem

The ASR‑Mars fleet must service multiple ARC‑Mars construction sites from multiple regolith excavation pits. This is formalised as a variant of the Traveling Purchaser Problem (TPP): given a set of demand nodes (construction sites, each requiring a specific tonnage per sol) and a set of supply nodes (excavation pits, each with a known regolith composition and extraction rate), find the set of routes that minimises total fleet energy consumption while satisfying all demand constraints.

The objective function is:

\min \sum_{i=0}^{n} \sum_{j=0}^{n} c_{ij} x_{ij} \tag{5.5}

subject to:

\sum_{j=1}^{n} x_{0j} = \sum_{j=1}^{n} x_{j0} = m \quad \text{(m rovers depart and return to base)}

\sum_{i=0}^{n} x_{ij} = 1 \quad \forall j, \qquad \sum_{j=0}^{n} x_{ij} = 1 \quad \forall i

\text{No subtours; capacity constraints at each pit and demand satisfaction at each site.}

The problem is NP‑hard; the AIADN solves it using the Lin‑Kernighan heuristic with Mars‑specific constraints (maximum slope 25°, obstacle avoidance, radiation‑safe corridors). For the Phase 1 settlement with two ASR‑Mars units and three excavation pits, the optimal solution converges in < 1 s on the AIADN processor.

