AIADN: Artificial Intelligence Autonomous Deployed Network

6.1 Distributed Control Architecture

The AIADN is the computational and coordination backbone that transforms three independent rover classes into a single, synchronised construction organism. Operating across a multi-layer network spanning rover-to-rover mesh, orbital relay, and Earth oversight, the AIADN must satisfy four conflicting requirements simultaneously: (i) provable correctness of distributed task execution, (ii) resilience to individual rover failures with graceful degradation, (iii) adaptation to changing environmental conditions through on-board learning, and (iv) tolerance of the 4–24 minute Earth–Mars communication latency that precludes real-time human intervention.

The architecture adopts a three-layer hierarchy inspired by the DualSynNet framework proposed by Niu et al. (2025) for interplanetary network control and the Fed-ASTRA scheduling architecture of Liu et al. (2025). Table 6.1 summarises the layers and their primary functions.

Table 6.1 — AIADN three-layer architecture.

Layer Location Function Update Rate
Layer 1: Rover Mesh On-board each rover Real-time state estimation, local task execution, obstacle avoidance 1 kHz
Layer 2: Base Server Foundry core / habitat hub Federated model aggregation, global task allocation, digital twin synchronisation Every 3 sols
Layer 3: Earth Oversight Deep Space Network High-level goal setting, contingency authorisation, anomaly review 1 Mbps, 4–24 min latency

Layer 1 operates as a peer-to-peer mesh network among all rovers within a 1 km radius, using 100 Mbps Ethernet over UHF relay. Each rover maintains a local state estimate \hat{\mathbf{x}}_i of the global construction progress and updates it via the consensus protocol described in Section 6.2.

Layer 2 resides on a radiation-hardened server in the Foundry Core, connected to all rovers via 1 Gbps optical fibre when docked. It aggregates local model updates via federated averaging, solves the global task allocation problem, and maintains the settlement digital twin.

Layer 3 provides the human-in-the-loop interface. The 4–24 minute round-trip latency prohibits continuous teleoperation but permits goal-level direction, anomaly adjudication, and periodic review of autonomous decisions. This mixed-initiative approach draws on the consensus-based bid intercession framework of Guillet et al. (2025), which enables human operators to influence task allocation without refactoring the underlying auction algorithm.

6.2 Consensus-Based State Estimation

Each rover i maintains a vector \hat{\mathbf{x}}_i \in \mathbb{R}^n representing its estimate of the global construction state: completed habitat units, steel inventory, regolith stockpiles, rover positions, and fault status. The consensus protocol updates this estimate at each communication round k according to:

\hat{\mathbf{x}}_i(k+1) = \hat{\mathbf{x}}_i(k) + \epsilon \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i} \left(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_j(k) - \hat{\mathbf{x}}_i(k)\right) \tag{6.1}

where \mathcal{N}_i is the set of rovers within communication range of rover i, and \epsilon \in (0, 1/\Delta_{\max}) is the consensus gain, with \Delta_{\max} being the maximum degree of the communication graph.

Theorem 6.1 (Consensus Convergence). Let the communication graph \mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}) be undirected and connected. Then under the update law (6.1), the state estimates of all rovers converge exponentially to the average of their initial values:

\lim_{k \to \infty} \hat{\mathbf{x}}_i(k) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{j=1}^{N} \hat{\mathbf{x}}_j(0), \quad \forall i \in \mathcal{V}

Proof. Define the disagreement vector \boldsymbol{\delta}_i(k) = \hat{\mathbf{x}}_i(k) - \bar{\mathbf{x}}(k), where \bar{\mathbf{x}}(k) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{j} \hat{\mathbf{x}}_j(k) is the instantaneous average. The consensus dynamics can be written in matrix form as \hat{\mathbf{X}}(k+1) = P \hat{\mathbf{X}}(k), where P = I - \epsilon L and L is the graph Laplacian. For a connected undirected graph, P has spectral radius \rho(P - \frac{1}{N}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^T) < 1, guaranteeing exponential convergence. ∎

For the MSAT deployment with rovers operating within a 1 km radius, the communication graph remains connected with probability exceeding 0.99 under normal operations. In the event of a rover failure that partitions the graph, the remaining connected component continues to converge on a local consensus, and the AIADN Layer 2 server re-establishes global consistency upon the next docking cycle.

Recent advances in Lyapunov-based stability analysis for multi-agent systems on general Lie groups (2025) extend this guarantee to the full 24-DOF pose estimation required for quadruped coordination, while event-triggered consensus schemes reduce communication load by transmitting state updates only when the estimation error exceeds a threshold.

6.3 Market-Based Task Allocation

The AIADN allocates construction tasks—excavation, material transport, printing, steel installation, inspection—using a distributed auction algorithm. Each task j is characterised by a tuple (T_j, \mathbf{p}_j, t_j^{\text{deadline}}, \mathbf{r}_j), where T_j is the task type, \mathbf{p}_j is the location, t_j^{\text{deadline}} is the latest acceptable completion time, and \mathbf{r}_j is a vector of resource requirements.

Each rover i computes a bid for task j according to:

b_{ij} = c_{ij}^{\text{energy}} + \lambda_t \cdot t_{ij}^{\text{travel}} + \lambda_e \cdot (1 - e_{ij}) \tag{6.2}

where c_{ij}^{\text{energy}} is the estimated energy cost (including battery State-of-Charge penalty), t_{ij}^{\text{travel}} is the travel time from the rover's current position, e_{ij} \in [0,1] is an expertise factor reflecting the rover's historical success rate on tasks of type T_j, and \lambda_t, \lambda_e are weighting coefficients tuned by the AIADN from operational data.

The allocation proceeds in rounds. In each round, the auctioneer (the Layer 2 base server, or a designated lead rover if the server is unreachable) announces the set of unallocated tasks. Rovers submit bids; the auctioneer assigns each task to the lowest bidder, and the winning rover updates its schedule. The process repeats until all tasks are allocated or no rover has remaining capacity.

This architecture aligns with the cost-effectiveness maximisation multi-round auction algorithm of Zhou et al. (2025), which integrates task fitness modelling with a distributed auction mechanism and an improved payment rule to ensure incentive compatibility. For tasks requiring heterogeneous capabilities—for example, a printing task that only the ARC‑Mars can perform—the bid space is restricted to capable rovers, consistent with the hybrid voting-based task assignment (HVBTA) framework of Weiner et al. (2025), which uses suitability matrices and multiple voting mechanisms to match heterogeneous agent capabilities to task requirements.

Theorem 6.2 (Task Completion Guarantee). If the total rover capacity exceeds the total task demand, and the communication graph remains connected, the auction algorithm assigns every task to a capable rover within a finite number of bidding rounds.

Proof Sketch. Each round reduces the number of unallocated tasks by at least one (the task receiving the lowest bid). Since the set of tasks is finite, termination is guaranteed. The connectivity assumption ensures that all capable rovers receive task announcements and can submit bids. ∎

6.4 Formal Correctness Guarantees via Supervisory Control Theory

While the auction-based allocation provides efficiency, it does not inherently guarantee that the target structure will be completed correctly regardless of the order in which robots execute their assigned tasks. For this strongest form of guarantee, the AIADN incorporates a formal methods layer based on Supervisory Control Theory (SCT).

Rosa et al. (2025) presented a formal modular synthesis approach for coordinating multiple robots to build 3-D structures. Their method models each robot as a discrete-event system and the target structure as a specification automaton. A supervisor is synthesised that is correct-by-construction: it permits only those sequences of robot actions that lead to completion of the structure while preventing deadlocks, collisions, and specification violations.

The AIADN adapts this approach as follows:

1. Modelling phase: The ARC‑8 habitat is decomposed into a set of atomic construction actions (extrude layer k, place block b, install tendon t, etc.). Each action has preconditions (material availability, rover position) and postconditions (state update).
2. Supervisor synthesis: Using the SPECTRUM toolchain, a monolithic supervisor is synthesised from the parallel composition of rover models and the structure specification. For computational tractability given the 27-sol construction horizon, the supervisor is computed offline on Earth and uploaded as a state machine.
3. Online execution: The supervisor communicates with the auction-based allocator. Tasks that pass the auction are submitted to the supervisor for clearance; the supervisor either authorises the task (if it is safe and contributes to progress) or withholds authorisation (if it would lead to a deadlock or violate precedence constraints).

This layered approach—formal supervisor for safety, market-based auction for efficiency—provides the best of both worlds: provable correctness and adaptive optimisation. For long-horizon tasks with time uncertainty, the complementary framework of White et al. (2025), based on generalised stochastic Petri nets with rewards, offers a path toward formal guarantees that account for the stochastic duration of excavation and printing operations.

6.5 Federated Learning Under Interplanetary Latency

The rovers must continuously improve their terrain classification, fault prediction, and task execution models using data collected during operations. Standard federated learning (FL) assumes low-latency parameter synchronisation, which breaks down under Earth–Mars propagation delays. The AIADN therefore implements a latency-tolerant FL scheme drawing on two recent advances.

DualSynNet (Niu et al., 2025). This framework deploys a federated multi-agent reinforcement learning architecture with graph attention (Fed-GAT-MADDPG) across an Earth–Mars dual-centre network. Centralised critics at Earth and Mars ground stations evaluate global reward, while decentralised actors on each rover make local decisions. Parameter synchronisation occurs via periodic model dispersal rather than continuous gradient exchange, exploiting the predictable orbital geometry of relay satellites. Simulation results demonstrate 52.4% task completion rates under realistic latency and link disruption profiles, with rover low-state-of-charge exposure constrained to approximately 0.8%.

Fed-ASTRA (Liu et al., 2025). This framework organises the deep-space network into a three-tier hierarchy—ground centres (Earth, Mars), orbital edge nodes, and rover terminal units. Federated reinforcement learning with environment-constrained action pruning (ECAP) filters out infeasible actions caused by link outages and energy thresholds, while a prioritised experience replay mechanism improves sample efficiency. The hierarchical organisation enables both local autonomy and cross-domain coordination, balancing rapidity (task timeliness) and availability (sustainable resource use).

AIADN Federated Averaging Protocol. For supervised learning tasks (terrain classification, fault diagnosis), the AIADN implements a modified FedAvg algorithm with the following Mars-specific adaptations:

1. Local training: Each rover trains its model on locally collected data for 3 sols (the inter-docking interval).
2. Staggered aggregation: Upon docking, rovers upload model weights to the Layer 2 base server. The server aggregates weights via:

\mathbf{w}^{t+1} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} \frac{n_i}{n} \mathbf{w}_i^{t+1} \tag{6.3}

where n_i is the number of local training samples and n = \sum_i n_i.

1. Earth synchronisation: Every 30 sols, the aggregated Mars model is transmitted to Earth. The Earth server fine-tunes the model on curated datasets and returns updated weights. The 4–24 minute latency is absorbed by the 30-sol synchronisation period; the rovers continue operating with the previous model until the update arrives.

Convergence under latency. Standard FL convergence proofs assume bounded gradient staleness. Let \tau_{\max} be the maximum round-trip time. For the AIADN scheme with synchronisation period T_{\text{sync}} = 30 sols, the effective staleness is \tau_{\max}/T_{\text{sync}} \ll 1 for all practical interplanetary distances, satisfying the bounded-staleness condition and preserving the \mathcal{O}(1/T) convergence rate for convex loss functions.

6.6 Fault Detection, Diagnosis, and Recovery

Long-term autonomy in the Martian environment demands active fault tolerance: the ability to detect, diagnose, and recover from hardware and software failures without human intervention. The AIADN implements a multi-layered FDDR (Fault Detection, Diagnosis, and Recovery) architecture.

Artificial Antibody Population Dynamics (AAPD) Model. O'Brien et al. (2025) introduced an immune-inspired model for detecting gradual degradation in robot swarms. The AAPD model maintains a population of "artificial antibodies," each tuned to recognise a specific fault signature (e.g., increased actuator current draw, degraded wheel encoder resolution, communication packet loss). Antibody concentrations rise in response to matching fault patterns and decay when the pattern is absent. The model reliably detects both gradual degradation (wear) and spontaneous changes (component failure) while remaining tolerant of normally behaving robots. In foraging robot experiments, swarms operating with AAPD maintained 70–97% of their performance under perfect conditions and prevented instances of field failure in most tested cases.

Predictive Maintenance via Weibull Survival Analysis. Complementing the reactive AAPD layer, a predictive maintenance module estimates the remaining useful life (RUL) of critical components using Weibull survival analysis. The hazard rate for component c follows:

h_c(t) = \frac{k_c}{\lambda_c} \left(\frac{t}{\lambda_c}\right)^{k_c - 1} \tag{6.4}

where k_c (shape parameter) and \lambda_c (scale parameter) are estimated from fleet-wide operational data. The RUL is computed as:

\text{RUL}_c = \lambda_c \cdot (\ln 2)^{1/k_c} - t_c^{\text{current}} \tag{6.5}

A 2025 comprehensive review of AI and robotics in predictive maintenance confirms that hybrid models combining Weibull-based empirical baselines with XGBoost or neural network residual learners achieve superior prognostic accuracy, reducing unexpected failures by up to 94% in industrial robotic systems.

Active Contact Response (ACR) for Physical Fault Recovery. When a rover suffers a catastrophic failure (e.g., leg actuator seizure, complete power loss), it becomes an obstacle to other rovers. Aina et al. (2025) proposed the Active Contact Response method, in which functional rovers leverage physical contact interactions to collectively reposition immobilised peers, reducing obstructions and maintaining group functionality. Experimental results demonstrate significantly improved recovery time from robot failures with minimal performance degradation during collective excavation tasks. The AIADN integrates ACR into the MEDEVAC protocol: when a rover is declared Level 4 (Catastrophic), the nearest functional rover is dispatched to tow or push the failed unit to a designated depot, clearing the worksite.

