6.7 Terrain Classification and Autonomous Path Planning

Safe and efficient rover navigation across unprepared Martian terrain requires real-time terrain classification and path planning that accounts for the nuanced difficulty of different surface types—from smooth bedrock to loose sand to boulder fields.

MarsTerrNet: Dual-Backbone Semantic Segmentation. A 2025 framework by the Chinese Academy of Sciences combines Progressive Residual Blocks (PRB) with a Swin Transformer to jointly capture fine-grained local details and global contextual dependencies in Martian terrain imagery. A feature-guided loss function encodes geological relations to reduce confusion between visually similar but physically distinct terrain types (e.g., dust-covered bedrock vs. exposed bedrock). MarsTerrNet achieves state-of-the-art performance on the MarsTerr2024 dataset, derived from Curiosity rover imagery, and produces geologically consistent segmentation results suitable for automated rover navigation.

