Layer 2: Base Server Connectivity. When a rover docks at the Foundry Core or a completed habitat, a 1 Gbps optical fibre connection is automatically established via the QSI-1 umbilical. During this docking window (typically 15–30 min for ASR material transfer, longer for ARC tool changes), the rover uploads its full data log, downloads updated models and schedules, and participates in federated aggregation.

Layer 3: Earth–Mars DTN. The Earth–Mars link operates through an orbital relay constellation (Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter-class assets) with a data rate of 1 Mbps. The Bundle Protocol (RFC 5050) provides store-and-forward delivery with custodial transfer. Bundles are prioritised: emergency beacons (S-band, 48 h independent battery) receive absolute priority; construction progress telemetry is sent at medium priority; and scientific data and model updates are sent at low priority, filling available bandwidth.

Latency impact on autonomy. Let t_{\text{prop}} be the one-way light-time (4–24 min). The ratio of latency to task duration is critical. For a 3-hour ASR transport cycle, t_{\text{prop}}/t_{\text{task}} \approx 0.02\text{--}0.13, meaning that Earth-based oversight can influence future cycles but not the current one. The AIADN therefore operates at autonomy Level 4 (supervised autonomy) pre-crew-arrival and transitions to Level 3 (task autonomy with human goal-setting) once the settlement is occupied. The hierarchical architecture of Fed-ASTRA, with regional control at Mars ground centres, is specifically designed to balance this latency-constrained autonomy with the benefits of cross-domain coordination.

