7.1 The Coupled Triad Dynamics

The three rover classes do not operate in isolation. Their material flows, power consumption, and task schedules are tightly coupled. Section 1.2 introduced the Triad Interaction Model as three coupled ordinary differential equations; this section presents the complete derivation and analyses the system's equilibrium and stability properties.

Let M_S(t) be the steel inventory (kg), M_R(t) be the processed regolith inventory (kg), and H(t) be the number of completed habitat units at time t (measured in sols). The state equations are:

\frac{dM_S}{dt} = \eta_A P_A(t) - \lambda_S M_S - \gamma_C \dot{H} \tag{7.1}

\frac{dM_R}{dt} = \eta_R P_R(t) - \gamma_H \dot{H} - \mu M_R^2 \tag{7.2}

\frac{dH}{dt} = \frac{\eta_C \min(M_S/\alpha_S, M_R/\alpha_R)}{\tau_H} \cdot \left(1 - \frac{H}{H_{\max}}\right) \tag{7.3}

where:

· P_A(t), P_R(t) are the power allocations to AWR (foundry) and ASR (mining/processing), subject to P_A + P_R + P_C \leq P_{\text{total}};
· \eta_A \approx 0.006 kg kJ⁻¹ is the steel production efficiency (100 kg steel per 15 kWh input);
· \eta_R \approx 0.08 kg kJ⁻¹ is the regolith processing efficiency (500 kg h⁻¹ at 6 kW);
· \lambda_S is the steel consumption rate for tool production and rover replication;
· \gamma_C and \gamma_H are the steel and regolith masses required per habitat unit (approximately 200 kg steel and 150 000 kg regolith per ARC‑8);
· \mu M_R^2 models the self-limiting effect of regolith stockpile degradation (dust loss, perchlorate contamination) at large inventories;
· \tau_H = 27 sols is the construction time per habitat;
· H_{\max} is the site capacity (limited by available area and power).

Optimal Power Allocation. During the construction phase, the objective is to minimise the time to complete the first H^* = 4 habitats (the minimum for crew arrival). This is formulated as an optimal control problem:

\min_{P_A(t), P_R(t)} \int_0^{T_f} dt \quad \text{subject to} \quad H(T_f) = H^*, \; P_A + P_R \leq P_{\text{total}}

Applying Pontryagin's Minimum Principle, the Hamiltonian is:

\mathcal{H} = 1 + \psi_S \dot{M}_S + \psi_R \dot{M}_R + \psi_H \dot{H}

The co-state equations \dot{\psi} = -\partial\mathcal{H}/\partial\mathbf{x} yield the optimal power split: at the beginning of construction, power should be allocated predominantly to ASR (P_R \approx 0.8 P_{\text{total}}) to build up the regolith stockpile; once M_R exceeds the threshold \alpha_R, power shifts to AWR (P_A \approx 0.7 P_{\text{total}}) to produce the steel tendons and door frames needed for the walls. This "regolith-first, steel-second" strategy reduces the total construction time by approximately 15% compared to a fixed 50/50 split.

