Molten Regolith Electrolysis upgrade. With the expanded power budget (1 MW+), the Phase 3 settlement commissions a Molten Regolith Electrolysis (MRE) plant as a parallel metals production pathway. MRE eliminates the need for carbon reductant by directly electrolysing molten regolith at approximately 1 600 °C, producing ferrosilicon alloys suitable for construction while releasing oxygen as a valuable by-product. The oxygen stream is integrated into the life support system, reducing the load on the solid-oxide electrolysis cells. The MRE pathway was advanced to TRL 3 at NASA Kennedy Space Center in 2025 and is projected to reach TRL 6 (Mars-relevant environment demonstration) by the Phase 3 deployment window.

7.5 Hexagonal City Planning: The Mathematical Rationale

The settlement grows according to a hexagonal master plan, a geometry chosen for its unique combination of structural, logistical, and psychological properties.

Structural rationale. A hexagonally close-packed arrangement of circular domes maximises the number of units that can be placed within a given perimeter while maintaining a minimum separation for radiation safety. For domes of radius R with a required separation d between exteriors, the packing density of a hexagonal lattice is:

\rho_{\text{hex}} = \frac{\pi R^2}{2\sqrt{3}(R + d/2)^2} \tag{7.4}

This exceeds the packing density of a square lattice by approximately 15%.

Logistical rationale. In a hexagonal grid with a central nexus, the average distance from any district to any other district is proportional to the grid radius rather than the diameter, reducing the mean travel time for both crew and robotic logistics by a factor of \sqrt{3}/2 \approx 0.87 compared to a rectangular grid of the same area.

Psychological rationale. A 2025 Space Syntax analysis of modular extraterrestrial habitats by Dalton et al. demonstrated that unplanned, accretive modular growth leads to declining spatial intelligibility and usability as settlements scale. The study introduced a vulnerability metric based on configurational redundancy, quantifying how module failure can fragment a habitat system, and confirmed an inverse relationship between structural resilience and spatial intelligibility. Their findings argue strongly for deliberate, informed planning interventions to sustain human-centred design at larger scales. The hexagonal MSAT layout, with its six radial arteries and redundant egress routes, directly addresses these concerns by providing multiple paths between any two points and ensuring that no single module failure can isolate a district.

7.6 Subsurface Development Strategy

While surface habitats with 2.5 m regolith shielding meet all radiation safety requirements, subsurface spaces offer additional advantages: zero radiation background, stable temperatures year-round, and protection from micrometeorites and surface dust storms. The MSAT settlement plan therefore includes a staged subsurface development strategy.

Lava tubes as natural shelters. Lava tubes—subsurface conduits formed by volcanic activity—have emerged as prime candidates for long-term extraterrestrial habitats. A comprehensive 2025 review in Space Science Reviews confirms that deep inflation and thermal/mechanical erosion along weak horizons is the most plausible formation mechanism for kilometre-scale tubes on Mars, and that the structural integrity and environmental stability of these tubes make them ideal for safe, long-term habitats offering natural protection from cosmic radiation, temperature extremes, and surface hazards.

The MSAT subsurface strategy includes:

· Depth 0–5 m: Surface habitats (ARC‑Mars standard construction).
· Depth 5–10 m: Critical infrastructure (power distribution, water storage, server farms) constructed by ARC‑Mars with excavation support.
· Depth 10–20 m: Radiation-free living and working spaces, accessed via pressurised vertical shafts. Construction employs the same sulfur-concrete lining technique but in a horizontal tunnelling configuration.
· Depth 20–50 m: Storage, backup life support, and emergency shelters. Where accessible lava tubes exist within a 10 km radius of the settlement, they are sealed with inflatable liners and connected to the surface via pressurised access tunnels.
· Depth 50 m+: Future expansion; the stable subsurface temperature (approximately −40 °C at 50 m depth on Mars) eliminates the need for active heating of storage volumes.

7.7 Digital Twin and Progress Monitoring

The AIADN maintains a continuously updated digital twin of the settlement—a virtual replica that mirrors the physical state of every habitat, rover, and infrastructure element in near-real-time. The digital twin serves three critical functions:

1. Progress monitoring: The twin compares the as-built state (captured by rover-mounted LIDAR and cameras during each docking cycle) against the as-planned state from the BIM (Building Information Model). Deviations exceeding ±5 mm trigger an automatic flag for inspection.
2. Predictive simulation: The twin runs Monte Carlo simulations of the construction schedule, sampling from empirical distributions of excavation rates, print speeds, and failure probabilities to forecast completion dates with 90% confidence intervals.
3. Human situation awareness: When the crew arrives, the digital twin provides an intuitive interface for monitoring settlement status without requiring physical inspection of every system.

The BIM2RDT framework of Akhavian et al. (2025) provides the technological foundation for this capability, bridging the gap between static BIM models and dynamic, robot-ready digital twins through semantic point cloud registration, LLM-reasoned alignment, and real-time safety event mapping. Integration of drone-based 3D scanning with BIM for automated construction progress control has been validated on terrestrial construction sites, demonstrating the feasibility of real-time deviation detection and mitigation.

