7.8.1 Solar Particle Event Response (GLE Protocol)

Detection of a ground-level enhancement (radiation > 100 mSv h⁻¹) triggers the following automated sequence:

T+0 AIADN broadcasts "Storm Warning" to all units; all rovers immediately cease non-essential motion
T+1 min ARC‑Mars executes "Hard Cap"—laser vitrifies the current print layer to seal the wall against dust ingress
T+2 min ASR‑Mars positions itself windward of the active construction site, acting as a physical radiation shield for the partially completed habitat
T+5 min All rovers enter "Hibernation Lock": joints mechanically locked, non-essential electronics powered down, EDS activated in continuous mode
Duration Maintain until radiation falls below 10 mSv h⁻¹ for 1 continuous hour
Recovery Post-storm structural scan (thermal delta analysis); re-heat and bond interface if cooling gradient > 50 °C cm⁻¹; resume printing from vitrified interface; estimated delay: 12–48 h

7.8.2 Dust Storm Operations (DUST-SHROUD Protocol)

Dust storms are classified into three phases based on atmospheric optical depth. The Adaptive Bayesian Ensemble Kalman Filtering (ABEKF) approach demonstrated in 2025 for rover trajectory planning during dust storms improves operational uptime by 25% during high-risk scenarios by dynamically reassessing plans based on evolving opacity forecasts.

Phase 1 (Visibility < 1 km): Rovers complete their current task segment and begin return to base; electrostatic dust shedding activated; non-critical optical surfaces covered.

Phase 2 (Visibility < 100 m): Rovers anchor to ground or nearest permanent structure; positive-pressure purge of sensitive avionics bays with filtered CO₂; entry into low-power hibernation mode; estimated downtime: 2–5 sols.

Phase 3 (Recovery): Self-cleaning cycle (vibration + EDS); full sensor calibration sequence; system health check before resuming normal operations.

7.8.3 Multiple Rover Failure (LAZARUS Protocol)

If more than 50% of the rover fleet becomes non-operational (any combination of Levels 3 and 4 failures), the LAZARUS protocol is invoked:

1. AIADN reassesses remaining capability and recalculates the construction timeline.
2. All remaining functional rovers are prioritised for habitat completion over expansion.
3. Functional AWR‑Mars units cease tool production and begin fabricating replacement rover components.
4. If < 30% of the fleet remains operational, an emergency resupply request is queued for the next Earth launch window.
5. Estimated recovery time: 90–180 sols to restore full fleet capability.

7.8.4 Complete Earth Communication Loss (HERMIT Protocol)

In the event of complete loss of Earth communication (orbital relay failure, DSN outage, or political disruption), the AIADN:

1. Assumes full autonomous control at Level 5 (full autonomy).
2. Continues according to the last approved construction plan, stored in triply redundant solid-state memory.
3. Stores all telemetry, science data, and model updates locally for eventual transmission upon link restoration.
4. Transmits a periodic S-band beacon indicating system status (nominal/degraded/emergency) and the number of habitable units.
5. Estimated autonomous operation duration without Earth contact: 2+ years, limited only by the supply of Earth-sourced consumables (DCPD, semiconductors).

