8.1 The Economics of ISRU: Closing the Cost Gap

The fundamental economic argument for the MSAT architecture rests on a single, stark cost differential: launching structural mass from Earth versus manufacturing it on Mars from locally sourced regolith. As the cost to send NASA's one-tonne Perseverance rover to Mars reached approximately US$243 million, the economics of shipping bulk construction materials—steel beams, concrete aggregate, radiation shielding—become prohibitive for any settlement exceeding a handful of astronauts. Dr Deddy Nababan, the CSIRO astrometallurgist whose experimental work validated the carbothermic reduction pathway central to the AWR‑Mars foundry, frames the issue succinctly: "Sending metals to Mars from Earth might be feasible, but it's not economical. Can you imagine bringing tonnes of metals to Mars? It's just not practical. Instead, we can use what's available on Mars. It's called in-situ resource utilisation, or ISRU".

The MSAT architecture transforms this economic equation by substituting Earth-launched mass with Martian-manufactured mass at a ratio exceeding 20:1 within five Mars years. This section quantifies that transformation, provides a net-present-value analysis, and compares the MSAT approach against traditional fully-imported habitat architectures.

