8.2 Mass Leverage Ratio and Exponential Growth

8.2.1 Definition and Derivation

The central economic metric for the MSAT architecture is the Mass Leverage Ratio L(t), defined as the mass of Mars-produced goods per unit mass of Earth-supplied goods:

L(t) = \frac{M_{\text{Mars}}(t)}{M_{\text{Earth}}}

The dynamics of L(t) are governed by a logistic growth model with self-replication feedback:

\frac{dL}{dt} = \alpha \cdot P(t) \cdot (1 + \beta L) \cdot \left(1 - \frac{L}{L_{\max}}\right) \tag{8.1}

where:

· \alpha = 0.02\ \text{kg kWh}^{-1} is the productivity coefficient (mass of goods produced per unit energy);
· P(t) is the total available power (kW);
· \beta = 0.1 is the self-replication feedback coefficient (the fraction of Mars-produced goods that contribute to expanding productive capacity);
· L_{\max} \approx 50 is the asymptotic limit set by the availability of rare-earth elements and semiconductor-grade silicon.

