8.3 Net Present Value and Return on Investment

8.3.1 Cost Model

The total program cost is modelled as:

C_{\text{total}} = C_0 + \sum_{k=1}^{K} \frac{C_{\text{annual}}}{(1+r)^k}

where:

· C_0 = \$15\ \text{billion} is the upfront development and first-mission cost;
· C_{\text{annual}} = \$1\ \text{billion} is the annual operating cost (Earth support, occasional resupply, DSN tracking);
· r = 0.07 is the discount rate (standard for long-horizon government infrastructure projects);
· K = 20\ \text{years} is the evaluation horizon.

The revenue/value stream R_t is the estimated economic value of the Mars-produced infrastructure, computed as the replacement cost of equivalent Earth-launched hardware:

R_t = M_{\text{Mars}}(t) \times C_{\text{launch}}

where C_{\text{launch}} = \$2\,300\ \text{kg}^{-1} (baseline scenario, Table 8.1).

8.3.2 NPV Analysis

The net present value is:

\text{NPV} = -C_0 + \sum_{t=1}^{T} \frac{R_t - C_{\text{annual}}}{(1+r)^t}

Figure 8.1 (not rendered here; refer to the thesis manuscript) presents the cumulative discounted cash flow for the three launch-cost scenarios. Under the baseline scenario:

· Year 5: NPV ≈ −$8 billion (investment phase, infrastructure not yet fully monetised);
· Year 10: NPV ≈ −$2 billion (approaching breakeven as mass leverage accelerates);
· Year 15: NPV ≈ +$12 billion (infrastructure replacement value exceeds cumulative investment);
· Year 20: NPV ≈ +$35 billion (settlement generating significant economic value).

Under the optimistic scenario (launch cost $500 kg⁻¹), NPV turns positive at approximately Year 10 and reaches +$50 billion by Year 20. Under the conservative scenario ($25 000 kg⁻¹), NPV never turns positive—reinforcing the critical dependence of the economic case on continued reduction in launch costs.

A 2025 analysis from Brookings notes that "as space-based industries have become more integrated into the economy, the projected commercial value has risen sharply," with the global space economy projected to grow at approximately 9% annually—well above global GDP growth. This macroeconomic context supports the assumption that launch costs will continue to decline and that the value of space-based infrastructure will appreciate.

8.4 Energy Return on Investment

The Energy Return on Investment (EROI) measures the energy payback of the MSAT system:

\text{EROI}(t) = \frac{E_{\text{out}}(t)}{E_{\text{in}}}

where:

· E_{\text{in}} = 750\ \text{TJ} is the energy required to manufacture and launch the Vulcan‑1 payload (25 000 kg at 30 MJ kg⁻¹ for launch to Mars transfer orbit);
· E_{\text{out}}(t) = M_{\text{Mars}}(t) \times 30\ \text{MJ kg}^{-1} is the embodied energy of the Mars-produced infrastructure (the energy that would have been required to launch equivalent mass from Earth).

