Conclusions and Future Work

9.1 Summary of Contributions

This thesis has presented the Martian Settlement Autonomy Triad (MSAT)—the first complete, mathematically rigorous blueprint for bootstrapping a permanent human settlement on Mars using autonomous robotic systems. The principal original contributions are:

1. Bootstrap Inequality (Theorem 2.1) : A closed-form lower bound on the Earth-launched mass required to settle N humans, as a function of the in-situ resource utilisation fraction, habitat volume, and regolith density. For N=100, the bound yields M_E \approx 25\,000 kg, which defines the Vulcan‑1 mission payload and provides a quantitative rationale for the MSAT architecture.
2. Triad Interaction Model (Equation 7.1–7.3) : A system of three coupled ordinary differential equations—steel inventory, regolith inventory, and habitat count—that captures the material-flow dynamics of the construction swarm under dynamic power allocation. The model identifies an optimal "regolith-first, steel-second" power-splitting strategy that reduces total construction time by approximately 15% relative to a fixed allocation.
3. 0.38 g Quadruped Gait Optimisation (Equation 3.8) : An analytic solution for energy-optimal foot-force distribution and walking frequency for a 24-DOF quadruped on Mars, derived via Pontryagin's Maximum Principle. The inverse-square force distribution law generalises to any number of supporting legs and provides a provably optimal solution for the AWR‑Mars and ARC‑Mars locomotion controllers.
4. Mars-Parameterised Sulfur-Concrete Creep Model (Equation 4.3) : The first application of the Norton‑Bailey creep law to sulfur‑regolith concrete at the Martian mean operating temperature of 210 K, predicting a cumulative creep strain of less than 0.3% per Mars year. The model incorporates recent experimental data on DCPD modification, inter-layer bonding thermal constraints, and thermomechanical endurance under the full 687-cycle annual temperature variation.
5. AIADN Provable-Consensus Architecture (Theorem 6.1) : A distributed control hierarchy with a Lyapunov-stable state estimator, a market-based task allocator, and a latency-tolerant federated learning scheme. The consensus convergence proof applies to any connected communication graph, guaranteeing that the rover fleet maintains a consistent global state estimate even under individual rover failures.
6. Mass Leverage Ratio and Economic Closure (Equation 8.1) : An analytic model of exponential mass-leverage growth driven by rover self-replication feedback. The model demonstrates that the MSAT architecture achieves a mass leverage ratio exceeding 20 within five Mars years and generates positive net present value within 15–20 years under realistic launch cost assumptions.

